Author: Danny Milosavljevic <dannym@friendly-machines.com>
Date: 2026-04-20
Subject: Pair stringdup's getmem with stringdispose's freemem in TStringQueue.Delete
License: expat

compiler/cobjects.pas's `stringdup' allocates a pstring with

    getmem(p, length(s)+1)

i.e. a variable-size getmem of just the byte the shortstring needs.
The matching deallocator is `freemem(p, length(p^)+1)', which the
companion helper `stringdispose' wraps.

`TStringQueue.Delete' instead released the same buffer with plain
`dispose(p^.data)'.  In Pascal, `dispose' on a typed pointer frees
`sizeof(typeref^)' bytes -- here `sizeof(string) = 256' -- via the
new/dispose pair.  Native FPC tolerated the mismatch because its
heap manager folds new/dispose and getmem/freemem onto the same
underlying allocator and the size argument is reconciled from the
heap header rather than the caller-supplied value.  Under stricter
allocators -- including the tp2cc C++ lowering, where new/delete
target the C++ operator new/delete and getmem/freemem target
malloc/free -- the size-mismatched and pair-mismatched call is a
real heap error: ASan reports it as
`alloc-dealloc-mismatch (malloc vs operator delete)'.

`TStringQueue.Done' already pairs the buffer correctly with
`stringdispose'; this just brings Delete in line.

--- a/compiler/cobjects.pas
+++ b/compiler/cobjects.pas
@@ -817,7 +817,15 @@
          prev^.next:=p^.next
         else
          first:=p^.next;
-        dispose(p^.data);
+        { p^.data was allocated by stringdup -> getmem(_, length+1).
+          The matching deallocator is freemem of the same size, which
+          stringdispose wraps. The original `dispose(p^.data)' worked on
+          native FPC only because its heap manager folds new/dispose and
+          getmem/freemem onto the same allocator; under stricter
+          allocators (or under tp2cc's C++ lowering, where new/delete
+          and malloc/free are separate) the size-mismatched dispose is
+          a real heap error. }
+        stringdispose(p^.data);
         dispose(p);
         Delete:=true;
         exit;
